<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Product basics</p>
  <h2>What people most often want to know</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>What is Best Hydrate?</h3><p>A lemon-flavoured powdered hydration mix intended to be mixed with water according to the current package label. It combines glucose with listed electrolyte ingredients in a measured drink format.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Does it contain magnesium?</h3><p>No. Magnesium is not listed in the current formula and is not represented as a current Best Hydrate ingredient.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Is it a therapeutic oral rehydration solution?</h3><p>No. Best Hydrate is a consumer hydration product and should not be equated with appropriately formulated therapeutic oral rehydration solution used to treat diarrhoeal dehydration.</p></article>
    <article><h3>How do I mix it?</h3><p>Mix <strong>1 scoop with 500 mL of water</strong>. That is one prepared serving. If a newer physical label differs, follow the package in hand.</p><a href="/products/lemonade-best-hydrate#directions">Read Directions →</a></article>
    <article><h3>What is in one prepared serving?</h3><p>One 500 mL serving provides <strong>701 mg sodium</strong> and <strong>388 mg potassium</strong>, along with glucose and the rest of the current labelled formula.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Can I use it every day?</h3><p>It can fit routine use when the current label and your overall diet allow. One serving already provides meaningful sodium, so daily use should be considered alongside food, other drinks and any fluid or electrolyte restrictions.</p></article>
    <article><h3>How do the sodium numbers compare with daily reference intakes?</h3><p>For generally healthy adults, the sodium Adequate Intake is 1,500 mg/day and the Chronic Disease Risk Reduction intake is 2,300 mg/day. One serving contains about 47% of the Adequate Intake. Those whole-day reference values are not a Best Hydrate servings-per-day target.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Can I use more to hydrate faster?</h3><p>Do not automatically stack servings or concentrate the powder. During prolonged heat or heavy sweating, fluid and sodium losses can rise, but replacement should reflect the situation and the current label rather than assuming more is always better.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section" aria-labelledby="ElectrolytePowderFaq-{{ section.id }}">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Electrolyte powder FAQ</p>
  <h2 id="ElectrolytePowderFaq-{{ section.id }}">Common questions about electrolyte powder</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>How often should you take electrolyte powder?</h3><p>There is no single schedule that fits everyone. Use depends on activity, heat, sweat losses, food, other drinks and the product label. Best Hydrate does not need to be used with every drink or every day.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Can you bring electrolyte powder on a plane?</h3><p>Powder is portable and can be useful for travel. Airline and airport screening rules can vary by country and quantity, so check the current rules for your departure airport and keep the product clearly labelled when practical.</p></article>
    <article><h3>How should electrolyte powder be stored?</h3><p>Keep the powder dry, close the container after use and follow the storage instructions on the current package. Avoid introducing wet utensils or moisture into the jar.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Does electrolyte powder have sugar?</h3><p>Some products do and some do not. Best Hydrate intentionally includes a small amount of glucose rather than being zero sugar. Check the current label for the exact prepared nutrition information.</p></article>
    <article><h3>How does electrolyte powder work?</h3><p>When mixed with water, electrolyte ingredients dissolve into ions such as sodium, potassium and chloride. These ions participate in normal fluid balance and other physiological functions. Product-specific effects still depend on the formula, dose and situation.</p><a href="/pages/hydration-science">Read Hydration Science →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Is electrolyte powder worth it?</h3><p>That depends on the situation. It can be a practical option when you want a portable, measured drink with electrolytes, but it is not automatically necessary for every person or every hydration occasion.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

